// onsa.toml
// [test]
// spec = ["§2.5", "§7"]

// Boundary of the nesting depth, blocks: nested `if` (an `if` and its block are two levels), an
// `else if` chain (each `else if`'s `if` is one level under the previous `if`), nested `match` (the
// arms are not levels, so one level per `match`), `for` and `while` loops (loop and body block are
// two levels).
//
// Levels are counted per declaration unit from 0 (spec 2.5): a function body is level 1, and each
// form (parenthesis, operator, postfix, keyword, block, type with arguments, pattern) is one level;
// names, literals, `_`, sequences and statements are not counted. Every case unit is exactly 257
// levels deep, one over the limit: each gets one E0006, and the marker is on the line of the token
// that makes level 257 (the first one in reading order). The helper declarations are at most 256
// deep and are accepted.
//
// if_nest_paren: the accepted unit ends with `(1)` (the `(` is level 256), the over one with `((1))`
// (the second `(` is level 257, the primary position). if_nest_block: its depth is odd (1 + 2k), so
// the accepted unit is 255 deep (the exception to the 256 above) and the over one 257: the `{` of
// the 128th then-block is level 257. else_if_chain: the `if` of the n-th link is level n + 1 and its
// block n + 2 (254 links are accepted); the conditions are names. match_nest: one level per `match`,
// the arms are not counted. for_nest and while_nest: 127 loops and the innermost `n = n + 1` (its
// `+` is level 256) are accepted; the over unit has 128 loops and the `{` of the 128th body is level
// 257. The loop `for i in xs` iterates a name, so no range is read before the body.

